.TH CONS 3
.SH NAME
cons, consctl, null, zero \- console device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #c /dev
.nf

.B /dev/cons
.B /dev/consctl
.B /dev/null
.B /dev/zero
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These four files are part of the one-level directory the
.B #c
device serves.
The rest of that directory is described on three other pages:
.IR fb (3)
for the raw screen,
.IR kbd (3)
for the raw scancode stream, and
.IR uart (3)
and
.IR mouse (3)
for the serial line and the pointer.
A single
.B bind
of
.B #c
at
.B /dev
is what puts all of them there together.
.PP
A write to
.B cons
draws the characters on the screen and sends them out the serial port, and
always takes the whole write: the console has no back pressure to report.
A read of
.B cons
parks until there is a line to hand back, unless the console is in raw mode,
in which case any byte answers it.
Whichever mode is in force,
.B \er
is translated to
.BR \en ,
because a reader should not have to know what kind of terminal typed at it.
.PP
Cooked mode, the default, is a line under construction that a reader never
sees until it is sent.
A printable character is appended to the line and echoed where it was typed.
Backspace and delete take the last character off the line and off the
screen.
Control\-U throws the whole line away and starts a fresh one.
A carriage return or newline sends the line, newline included.
Control\-D sends the line with no newline when there are characters in it,
and marks an end of file when there are not: reading
.B cons
answers zero bytes exactly then, and nowhere else.
Every other control character, and every byte outside the printable range,
is refused rather than stored: the line discipline has no way to show a
reader a byte it cannot spell as a printable character.
Control\-C throws the line away and posts
.B interrupt
to the process group last known to be reading the console (see
.IR intro (1)).
.PP
Raw mode, reached through
.BR consctl ,
is what is left when the line discipline is taken out: every byte reaches a
reader as it arrives, none of the characters above mean anything, and
nothing is echoed.
It is what a program that draws its own input, or reads a password, needs.
Switching between raw and cooked mode discards whatever line was under
construction, because those characters were typed under rules that no
longer apply.
.PP
.B Consctl
takes one command per write.
.TP 8
.B rawon
raw mode, with echo off
.TP
.B rawoff
cooked mode, with echo on
.TP
.B echoon
turn echo on without changing the mode
.TP
.B echooff
turn echo off without changing the mode
.PD
.PP
A command nothing above recognises is refused with
.BR EINVAL ,
and none of the write is taken.
The last
.B consctl
fid closed puts the console back in cooked mode with echo on, so a program
that turns raw mode on and then ends abnormally does not leave a console
nobody can type at.
.PP
Reading
.B consctl
answers with the commands that would put the console back in its current
state, one per line, for example
.IP
.EX
rawoff
echoon
.EE
.PP
Unlike Plan 9's
.BR consctl ,
this file can be read.
A program can save what it reads and write it back later, and a check has
something exact to compare its own commands against.
.PP
The
.B null
file discards whatever is written to it and answers zero bytes on every
read, which is an end of file.
The
.B zero
file discards whatever is written to it and answers a buffer of zero bytes
on every read, without limit.
Both writes always take the whole count.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR fb (3),
.IR kbd (3),
.IR uart (3),
.IR mouse (3),
.IR intro (1),
.IR kill (1),
.IR read (2),
.IR notify (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A read of
.B cons
that is still unanswered when the connection abandons it fails with
.BR EINTR .
Every other operation these four files refuse answers
.B EPERM
for an operation they do not have, such as a write to a read\-only file, or
.B EISDIR
for a message meant for a directory.
.SH BUGS
There is no word erase and no command history: control\-U is the only way
to take back more than the last character.
.PP
There is one console for the whole machine.
A write to
.B consctl
changes cooked or raw mode, and echo, for every reader of
.B /dev/cons
at once.
